{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-32639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040095/state.json" "file_sha256" "a52e91968bb249d67ea869beeb52d161a3c13d1f610c18a436cfd4e6b842442b"} {"question" "q-32639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6y-1)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040095/state.json" "file_sha256" "a52e91968bb249d67ea869beeb52d161a3c13d1f610c18a436cfd4e6b842442b"} {"question" "q-32639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.d